      "restoredAt": null,
      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{6743d520219c48d672a2181c|full_name}, world creators don't have to take it into their own hands though. The \"FISH!\" world didn't intentionally break the Creator Guidelines and make a blacklist when they had similar issues. They just warned the users about it while they fixed the issue. (But the issue was of course easier to fix for them because that world is basically singleplayer.)\n\nThe creator could easily have just added a sign in their world saying that public instances are being targeted by malicious users, and encouraging people to use private instances for a while.\n\nYou can't break the rules to punish other rule breakers, otherwise you get punished too.\n\nI am not saying the Creator Guidelines are perfect, but you definitely shouldn't intentionally violate them and then complain about being punished for it.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
